Q38. Explain SOLID Principles ?
👉 SOLID is a set of 5 design principles in object-oriented programming.
a. S – Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) : 
                      - A class should do only one job. If one class is doing multiple jobs, changes in one part may break another.
                      - eg - Controller is only responsible for API endpoints, not business logic. etc
 eg :
@RestController
public class UserController {
    @PostMapping("/users")
    public String saveUser() {
        return userService.saveUser();
    }
}



b. O – Open/Closed Principle (OCP) :
                      - Code should be open for extension, closed for modification. Means: add new features without changing old code.
                      - eg- In Spring Boot, we achieve this using interfaces + polymorphism + dependency injection.
eg :
public interface PaymentService {
    void pay();
}

@Service
public class CreditCardPayment implements PaymentService {
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Credit Card Payment");
    }
}

@Service
public class UpiPayment implements PaymentService {
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("UPI Payment");
    }
}


c. L – Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) :
                      - Child class should be able to replace the parent class without changing program behavior.
                      - eg - In Spring Boot, we achieve it by designing interfaces with proper contracts and making sure all implementations behave consistently.
eg :
public interface NotificationService {
    void send();
}

@Service
public class EmailNotificationService implements NotificationService {
    @Override
    public void send() {
        System.out.println("Email Sent");
    }
}

@Service
public class SmsNotificationService implements NotificationService {
    @Override
    public void send() {
        System.out.println("SMS Sent");
    }
}

@RestController
public class NotificationController {
    @Autowired
    private NotificationService notificationService; // Parent reference is calling child implementation
    @GetMapping("/send")
    public String sendNotification() {
        notificationService.send();
     return "Notification Sent";
    }
}
Note:- so here any child replacing the parent interface safely.



d. I – Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) :
                      - Don’t force classes to implement methods they don’t use. Better to have small specific interfaces than one big interface.
                      - eg- In Spring Boot, we achieve it by designing modular service interfaces.
eg :
public interface Workable {
    void work();
}

public interface Eatable {
    void eat();
}

public interface Sleepable {
    void sleep();
}

public class Developer implements Workable, Eatable, Sleepable {
    public void work() {
        System.out.println("Writing Code");
    }
    public void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }
    public void sleep() {
        System.out.println("Sleeping");
    }
}

public class Robot implements Workable {
    public void work() {
        System.out.println("Working");
    }
}


D – Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP):
                      - Classes should depend on interfaces, not concrete classes. This makes code flexible and testable.
                      - eg - In Spring Boot, we achieve DIP naturally using interfaces + dependency injection.
eg :
@Autowired
private NotificationService notificationService;
